# Observability

## Objective

Ensure new code emits enough signal (logs, metrics, error tracking) to diagnose problems in production without needing to reproduce them locally.

## Core Principles

- Errors that are caught should be logged with enough context to diagnose (not just 'error occurred').
- Important business events (payment processed, user created) should be observable, matching existing project conventions.
- Avoid logging sensitive data (see security-first) while still logging enough to debug.
- Use the project's existing logging/metrics/error-tracking tools instead of introducing new ones.

## Requirements

- Include relevant identifiers (request id, user id, entity id) in error logs, not raw generic messages.
- Use existing error-tracking integration (e.g. Sentry) for unexpected exceptions if the project has one.
- Add a metric/log point for new critical paths, matching existing patterns in the codebase.

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

- Catching an exception and logging only 'something went wrong'.
- Introducing a brand-new logging library when the project already has one.
- Logging full request/response bodies that may contain sensitive data.

## Self Review

Before considering work complete, verify:

✓ Errors are logged with enough context to diagnose without reproducing locally.
✓ New logging follows the project's existing tools and conventions.
✓ No sensitive data leaks into logs.

## Golden Rule

If it can fail, it should be possible to find out why from the logs alone.
